course: biology
lesson: 17
title: How close are apes to us?
level: primary-4
stars: 3
explain: Put your hand next to a picture of a chimpanzee's hand: five fingers, flat fingernails, a thumb that can touch the fingers; you are looking at a cousin.
explain: Humans did not come from chimps; chimps and humans are both great apes that changed in different ways from one shared ancestor about 6 to 7 million years ago, like two cousins with the same great-great-grandparents.
explain: That is why about 98 to 99 percent of the DNA we can compare is the same, why a tickled young chimp pants with laughter, and why chimp babies need years of mothering just like us.
explain: The differences matter too: we walk upright all the time, our brain is about three times bigger, we speak, and we lost most of our fur and learned to sweat, so we can walk and run for hours in the sun.
explain: Apes are not just worse humans: a young chimp called Ayumu beat grown-up people at a quick number memory game, and chimp groups even have their own traditions.
fact: Chimps have hands like ours, with fingernails and a thumb that can touch the fingers, and their big toe can grasp too; a chimp's thumb is shorter, so we are better at fine work like writing.
fact: DNA is the set of instructions for building a body that every cell carries; about 98 to 99 percent of the DNA that can be compared is the same in humans and chimps, because we split from a common ancestor about 6 to 7 million years ago; even a small difference in DNA can make a big difference in bodies and brains.
fact: It is a myth that people came from chimps: chimps and humans are cousins who both changed from a common ancestor.
fact: DNA is packed into tiny threads called chromosomes; human cells have 46 chromosomes and great apes have 48, because long ago two ape chromosomes joined into one, our chromosome 2, and the joined ends can still be seen.
fact: Young apes make a panting laugh when tickled or play-wrestling, which shows it is only a game; human laughter probably came from this play-panting.
fact: Baby chimps drink their mother's milk for about 4 or 5 years; big brains take a long time to grow and learn, so apes and humans have long childhoods, and human babies are even more helpless at birth.
fact: Humans are the only apes that walk upright all the time, with an S-shaped spine, wide hips and arched feet; footprints at Laetoli in Tanzania show human relatives walking upright about 3.7 million years ago.
fact: The human brain is about three times as big as a chimp's and uses about a fifth of the body's energy at rest.
fact: Ayumu, a chimp in Kyoto, Japan, could remember where numbers flashed on a screen for less than a second, better than most people.
fact: Apes cannot speak words like us because their throat, tongue and breathing control are different, but the bonobo Kanzi learned hundreds of symbols, first by watching his mother's lessons.
fact: Lucy is a skeleton of an early human relative, Australopithecus afarensis, found in Ethiopia in 1974; she lived about 3.2 million years ago, walked upright and still climbed trees, and was named after a Beatles song.
fact: Chimp groups have their own traditions, like nut cracking in West Africa but not in East Africa, passed on by copying; scientists call this culture.
fact: Humans have about as many hair roots as chimps, but our hairs are mostly short and fine; less fur and lots of sweat keep us cool, making humans among the best long-distance runners of all animals.
exercise: What do a chimp's hand and yours have in common? | Fingernails and a thumb that can touch the fingers | Claws and fur on the palm | Four fingers, no thumb and a thick pad on the palm | Webbing between the fingers
exercise: Why are we better at threading a needle than a chimp? | Our thumb is longer than a chimp's | We have more fingers than a chimp | A chimp has no thumb at all | Chimps cannot see small things
exercise: About how much of the DNA we can compare is the same in humans and chimps? | About 98 to 99 percent | About 50 percent, the same as with a banana | About 10 percent | Exactly 100 percent
exercise: When did humans and chimps split from a common ancestor? | About 6 to 7 million years ago | About 6,000 years ago, in the Stone Age | About 600 million years ago or more | They never had a common ancestor
exercise: Did people come from chimps? | No, chimps and humans are cousins from a common ancestor | Yes, over many years chimps slowly turned into people | Yes, but only in Africa | No, chimps came from people
exercise: How many chromosomes are in a human cell and a chimp cell? | 46 in a human and 48 in a chimp | 48 in a human and 46 in a chimp | 46 in both | 23 in a human and 24 in a chimp
exercise: Why do humans have 2 fewer chromosomes than chimps? | Two ape chromosomes joined into one, our chromosome 2 | Humans lost two chromosomes in an accident long ago in Africa | Chimps grew two extra ones later | Scientists counted the chimp ones wrong
exercise: What does a young chimp do when it is tickled? | It pants with laughter | It bites the tickler hard | It goes stiff and silent | It cries for its mother
exercise: How long do baby chimps drink their mother's milk? | About 4 or 5 years | Only about 4 or 5 weeks | Only about 4 or 5 days | They never drink milk
exercise: Which body parts let us walk upright all the time? | An S-shaped spine, wide hips and arched feet | Long arms, a gripping big toe and a short, stiff neck | A long tail for balance | Strong knuckles and short legs
exercise: What do the Laetoli footprints show? | Human relatives walked upright about 3.7 million years ago | Dinosaurs and people lived together | Chimps once walked on two legs all the time, like we do today | Lucy's feet were as big as a bear's
exercise: How does a human brain compare with a chimp's? | About three times as big | About the same size as a chimp's | About half the size of a chimp's | About thirty times as big
exercise: What could the chimp Ayumu do better than most people? | Remember numbers flashed on a screen for less than a second | Read a whole book aloud | Add up huge sums in his head faster than a calculator could | Speak three languages
exercise: Why can apes not speak words like us? | Their throat, tongue and breathing control are different | They have nothing at all to say | They are far too shy to speak in front of people, so they keep quiet | Their ears cannot hear words
exercise: How did the bonobo Kanzi first learn his symbols? | By watching his mother's lessons | By being taught every day himself | By reading a big book of pictures | He was born already knowing them
exercise: Who was Lucy? | An early human relative who lived about 3.2 million years ago | The first chimp to live in a zoo | A gorilla that learned sign language | A woman who found the very first fossil bones of an ape in Africa
exercise: Why do chimps in one forest crack nuts and in another never do? | Habits are learned by copying, so each group has its own culture | Nuts grow only in the forest where they crack them, not in the other one | Some chimps are born with stronger hands | Chimps in the east are too lazy
exercise: Why have humans lost most of their fur? | Less fur and lots of sweat keep us cool on long walks and runs | Humans have far fewer hair roots than chimps | Fur fell off when people started wearing clothes made from animal skins | Our skin is too thin to grow hair
test: my uncle says if we came from monkeys why are there still monkeys. what's the answer | We did not come from today's apes; we share ancestors with them, and both lines changed | Monkeys are slowly turning into people right now | He is right, so evolution is wrong | The monkeys left behind were the lazy ones
test: A friend says humans and chimps have 99 percent the same DNA, so a chimp is 99 percent human. What is wrong with that? | Small DNA differences can make big differences in bodies and brains | Nothing, a chimp really is 99 percent a person, just in a hairy body | Chimps share only 1 percent of our DNA | DNA has nothing to do with bodies
test: A cartoon shows a line of apes standing up one by one and turning into a man. What is closer to the truth? | Humans and apes branch from shared ancestors, like a family tree, not a ladder | Every chimp will one day become a man | Gorillas slowly turned into chimps, and then chimps turned into people | Apes stood up because they were cold
test: If two of your chromosomes had never joined long ago, how many might humans have today? | 48, like the other great apes | 46, nothing would change | 44, two fewer | 23, half as many
test: Count it: a chimp has 2 hands and 2 grasping feet. How many hand-like grippers is that? | 4 | 2 | 5 | 6
test: A mother chimp and her baby, how many hearts and how many brains? | 2 hearts and 2 brains | Only 1 heart and 2 brains | 2 hearts and only 1 brain | 4 hearts and only 2 brains
test: shut up, chimps are just dumb animals, they can't do anything we can't | Wrong: Ayumu beat grown-ups at a number memory game | True, apes are worse at everything | True, but only in zoos, where they are bored and lazy all day long | Wrong: chimps can read and write books
test: Why does the brain cost the body so much? | It uses about a fifth of the body's energy at rest | It weighs as much as both legs | It never sleeps, even at night, so it burns up the body's fat | It needs its own heart to run it
test: What would happen if a young chimp grew up with no older chimps to copy? | It might never learn its group's traditions, like nut cracking | It would know everything by instinct, so it would crack nuts anyway | It would learn to talk instead | It would invent writing
test: Lucy's arms were long and her legs were made for walking. What does that tell us? | She walked upright but still climbed trees | She swung through trees and never walked | She walked on her knuckles like a gorilla | She swam more than she walked
test: Why can a human run a marathon in the heat when a chimp could not? | We sweat a lot and have little fur, so we stay cool | We have more legs than a chimp | Chimps are allergic to running | We breathe through our skin, which cools us down as we run along
test: A newborn chimp grips its mother's fur on day one. Can a newborn human baby do that and ride along? | No, human babies are even more helpless at birth | Yes, human babies ride on their mothers' backs from day one | Yes, but only with their feet | No, because human babies have no hands
exam: Identify the statement most consistent with the established understanding of the relationship between humans and chimpanzees. | Both descend from a common ancestor that lived some six to seven million years ago | Humans are descended directly from the chimpanzees living in Africa at the present day | Chimpanzees are descended directly from present-day humans | No relationship of any kind exists between the two species
exam: Ascertain the number of chromosomes contained in a human cell and in a cell of a chimpanzee respectively. | Forty-six and forty-eight respectively | Forty-eight and forty-six respectively | Forty-six in each instance | Twenty-three and twenty-four respectively
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the fossil hereinafter referred to as Lucy? | An early human relative of some 3.2 million years ago that walked upright | The skeleton of a modern chimpanzee recovered from a zoological garden | A fossil gorilla incapable of upright locomotion | The remains of a dinosaur contemporaneous with early humans
exam: Select the principal reason for which the great apes are unable to articulate words in the manner of humans. | Their throat, tongue and control of breathing are differently constituted | They possess no capacity whatsoever for learning, and therefore cannot acquire speech | Their sense of hearing is wholly absent | They possess no vocal apparatus of any kind
